-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-
       $VER: CNet Milles (Mille Bornes) HISTORY, v1.18 File by Dotoran!
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

>>==--> v1.18 <--==<<

    Released: April 8, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - If an Amiga Player was showing a ROLL card, which it played on top
          of a STOP card, if it had a Right of Way card when you played a
          Speed Limit Card on, it would Coup Fourre you, as it should, but 
          at the same time it would "clear" it's Battle Pile, in addition to
          it's Speed Pile. Since the Amiga was showing a ROLL, it would then
          uncover the STOP it had played against it in a previous turn. This
          would cause that Amiga player to get into a loop where it could no
          longer play anything GOOD on top of itself, as it couldn't play
          Mileage, because it had a STOP card showing, but if it tried to
          play a ROLL, it couldn't, because it had the Right of Way card.

        - I believe that is the LAST bug contained in the game! At least, I
          haven't been alerted to any OTHER bugs! ;-)

    Changes/Additions:

        - Ruth Evans asked that I include a log entry within the game, so
          that she'd get a better idea as to when the game was used. This
          was added. If you are using an ARexxSays log, the entry will be
          placed there, else you'll see it in your "calls" log.

>>==--> v1.17 <--==<<

    Released: March 4, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - If you had MORE THAN ONE olm message waiting for you in a game,
          this would cause an error to be printed, while letting you continue
          with your game.

        - Some instances of the word "Milles" were changed to "Mille" in the
          online documentation file. These were fixed in the GAME previously,
          but I forgot to check the doc file.

    Changes/Additions:

        - The High Scores were changed slightly to allow for multiple SAME
          scores to exist on the list, provided the score BEATS at least one
          score ON the list. (If the list contains 10 instances of the SAME
          score and the player obtains the same score him/herself, their
          score will NOT appear, but if there is at least ONE score in the
          list that is LOWER than the players score, they'll be added).

        - Well, at least ONE person let me know they discovered one of the
          player secrets in the game, that being that it was advantageous to
          play the role of the 3rd player(or 6th player in a 6-Player game),
          because of the way the Amiga Players played their Hazard Cards. I
          told him that I was waiting until someone discovered that tactic
          before I remedied the situation, so you'll all be HAPPY to hear
          that you really don't have an advantage sitting in ANY seat, as the
          chance you'll get hit with an Amiga-played Hazard card is now an
          equal 50/50 split, vs. the 30/70 previously used. (To protect the
          innocent, I will refrain from mentioning his name! hehe)

        - The game now resets the WHERE text upon exiting the game, so it
          doesn't look like the user is still in the game after they've left
          the game(as there are areas within CNet where this text is not
          updated on a regular basis).

        - There is a new "breakinF" variable at the start of the "CNetMilles"
          command file. If you opt to set this variable to "0", then the
          BREAKIN mode will NOT be available during Human versus Amiga type
          games. (This will dramatically increase performance when run on
          multi-line systems, as the file isn't sitting within a do-loop for
          extended periods of time). The BreakIn mode will ALWAYS be available
          if the user opts to watch an Amiga vs. Amiga game, even if they opt
          to "Sit-In" after the game has played a bit.

>>==--> v1.16 <--==<<

    Released: February 1, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - The name "ray Amiga" wasn't capitalized!
        - When initiating a QuickStart game, there were times when it would
          neglect to ask if you wanted to "continue to 5000" miles.
        - When a QuickStart game is initiated, if you chose to SAVE a game,
          the save slots weren't initialized.
        - Ok, I think, FINALLY, I found the problem in the Amiga's usage of
          the RoW card. Please let me know if it ain't so! :-)
        - The "Break-In" mode is disabled when the "AutoFinish" hand is in
          process, as this caused numerous problems due to the fact that a
          "human" player is not in control during "AutoFinish".
        - If an OLM was waiting for you and you hit "S" to adjust your speed,
          the OLM would break in on you, disrupting the screen display. OLM's
          are now disabled while adjusting the speed.
        - It was possible to enter Non-Numeric values for the SPEED.
        - If you pressed "D", followed by ENTER, the "Oops" text was printing
          to the far LEFT of the screen, instead of right after the prompt.

    Changes/Additions:

        - The [O]LM commaand has been expanded:

            - If an OLM message is waiting for you, pressing "O" or "." will
              allow you to read/respond to it as per previous versions.
            - If no OLM is waiting, you can press "O" or "." to create and
              send an OLM yourself. When finished, you'll be taken back into
              the game. Any responses to your sent OLM will activate the
              "OLM Waiting" text in the lower right corner.

        - If there are SAVED games, you are now presented with the RELOAD
          Menu BEFORE any other game screens are displayed INCLUDING the
          Title Screen. When using "QuickStart" mode, if there are SAVED
          games, you'll be presented with the RELOAD Menu, however if you
          opt NOT to continue a SAVED game, then your QuickStart game will
          be initialized instead.

>>==--> v1.15 <--==<<

    Released: January 25, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - There was a variable conflict with the new smart path coding that
          resulted in extraneous files being created in the user's mail
          directory.

        - Numerous instances of "partners" were changed to "partner's", the
          correct grammatical sense. :-)

        - Another RoW bug was found and fixed, hopefully.
      
>>==--> v1.14 <--==<<

    Released: January 24, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - The High Score files were not being read into the game when a
          QuickStart game was started, which resulted in errant data being
          displayed to screen in the event you obtained a High Score.

        - The Speed Setting wasn't being "remembered" as it was supposed to.
          I changed the display routine in such a way that it now WILL keep
          the speed the same, as well as purposely slowing the display down
          for things like "end of game" wrap ups, etc.

        - Another RoW fix. This time when it's played as a Coup against a
          Speed Limit when the attackee also has a STOP. It removed the
          Speed Limit, but not the STOP. Now BOTH are removed.

        - A couple of you let me know how unpleased you were that I swapped
          the "0"/Discard with the ".". I've switched BACK now:

            - To discard, press "!" thru "&", or press "D" or press "0".
            - To check OLM's, press "O" or press ".".

          You'll be DISCARDING more often than checking for OLM's, so going
          back to the "0" (which is a physically larger key on the keyboard
          is probably a good idea after all)! hehe :-)

        - CNet, v4.24g was released and AGAIN the GetUsers changed.

        - The "smart path" used in this version uses the PARSE SOURCE ARexx
          command, instead of a long offset getuser!


>>==--> v1.13 <--==<<

    Released: January 12, 1997!

    Bug Fixes:

        - It was possible for you to press "F" when the command was NOT able
          to be used. Because of this, an error was produced which exited the
          game prematurely. The same error existed when trying to use the "O"
          command when no olm messages were waiting either.

        - With the addition of the new "F" command, coupled with the "O"
          command being in effect, there wasn't enough room on the bottom
          line to display the entire prompt. This resulted in stray characters
          appearing at the end of the bottom line of the display. The NAME of
          the current player was moved UP a line, which should help out with 
          any of the spacing problems that may have been evident.

        - More fixes to the Amiga Hazard Playing routines. (This is getting
          to be REAL fun! hehe) It may have been these problems which made
          the game appear to be harder to win at.

    Changes/Additions:

        - The game now takes ARGUMENTS! If you add a MIL command to BBSMENU,
          the user's can now specify up to FOUR arguments to launch a QUICK
          START game. The arguments, in order of sequence are as follows:

            - 1st - How many players in the game, between 2 and 6, NOT 5.
                    If the 1st argument is a ?, /, or the first letter is an
                    "H" (as in MIL HELP), a help screen will be displayed in
                    lieu of starting a game.

            - 2nd - Which player the user would like to play as. If only the
                    above argument is specified, then user will play the game
                    as Player # 1.

            - 3rd - Speed of Game setting. If present, the Game Speed will be
                    preset to this value before starting the game. If this
                    argument is NOT specified, then the built-in default speed
                    will be used. If the 3rd argument is a Y or N, the default
                    speed will be used, and this argument will be used as the
                    value of the 4th argument below.

            - 4th - Either a Y or N for whether or not the user wishes to
                    play their partners hand as well as their own. This arg
                    will only be looked at if the 1st arg is 4 or 6. If a 4-
                    or 6-player game and this argument is NOT specified, the
                    user will NOT play their partners hand either.

          If NO arguments are specified, or if the 1st argument is NOT a valid
          value, then a regular game will be initiated, with all the internal
          initial prompts being used. 

        - The "Pre-Game" help screens have been expanded to also tell the
          user about the QUICKSTART, OLM and FINISH commands. Because this 
          resulted in the need to create a second HELP screen, the user is 
          now ASKED if they wish to view the "Pre-Game" help screens, with 
          a default answer of NO being used.

        - Some Numeric Keypad commands were switched around:

                0 and O now look for OLM messages.
                . and D now ask for Discards.
                8 and F now ask for Hand Finish.

          After looking it over, I thought the above sounded better, since
          0 was similar to an O and the . stands for DELETE on the keypad.
          The 8 had to be changed to make the others work.

>>==--> v1.12 <--==<<

    Released: January 11, 1997! (Limited ALPHA release in IRC only)

    Bug Fixes:

        - The Amiga Players had the ability to play Hazard cards on TOP of
          other Hazard cards when they weren't supposed to.

        - If you tried to play a Remedy when you had the corresponding
          Safety card, it'd tell you you didn't need to play a ROLL card,
          even if the Remedy you chose was NOT a ROLL card.

    Changes/Additions:

        - The "speed" setting introduced in the 1.11 version would reset
          itself at the beginning of every game. It will now remember the
          speed setting and continue to use it for the entire duration you
          are IN the game.

        - Also pertaining to the SPEED, when you choose to CHANGE the speed,
          you'll be shown the current Speed Setting, which will be used as
          the default should you press RETURN/ENTER without entering a valid
          numeric value.

        - Also pertaining to speed, the setting will only be used during
          actual GAME PLAY, but will be disabled AFTER a HAND or GAME has
          been completed, so the user will be able to READ what the "end of
          game" prompts are saying.

        - Concerning the internal "amigascores" variable, a check is now
          performed in PARTNER games, so that if a user is playing a PARTNER
          game and their PARTNER is an Amiga Player and it's this Amiga
          Player that plays the winning points, the score WILL NOW be able
          to be placed on the High Scores sheets, even if the "amigascores"
          variable is set to 0.

        - Added a new command, Finish, which comes into play only AFTER the
          Draw Pile contains no more cards. When this occurs, if you see that
          there really isn't anything else you can do to help yourself, you
          can opt to press "F" (or "0" on the keypad) to have the GAME itself
          automatically finish the hand for you(by temporarily assigning some
          Amiga players to control your Human players). You'll be able to
          pick up with the start of the next hand.

>>==--> v1.11 <--==<<

    Released: December 22, 1996!

    Bug Fixes:

        - If an Amiga Player plays a Hazard on someone with a Right of Way
          card, the resulting Coup Fourre would remove ANY Hazard card that
          player had on their Battle Pile, instead of just a STOP Card.

        - If you SAVED a game in the MIDDLE of a Hand, when you resumed that
          game, you would NOT get the points earned FOR that or future Hands.

        - If you SAVED a game after the completion of a Hand where an Amiga
          player won the hand, and it was a hand where the Amiga player opted
          NOT to play the Extension, depending on WHICH Amiga Player it was
          that WON the hand, there was the possibility that when you RESUMED
          the game, that player would opt TO play the extension(thus they
          changed their minds?). This affected the scoring again, so whatever
          decision the Amiga Player makes initially will get SAVED to the
          SAVED GAME files, so this type of problem won't occur again!

        - If you SAVED a 4-Player game, where YOU controlled the PARTNERS
          hand, in addition to your OWN, the NAME used in the SAVED GAME menu
          file was NOT your name, but the name of one of the Amiga Players.
          YOUR name was saved in the actual GAME file, and you COULD continue
          playing the partner's hand, but when viewing the list of SAVED
          GAMES, it wasn't being displayed correctly.

    Changes/Additions:

        - Added an "S" (or "9" on the keypad) command to allow the user to
          adjust the SPEED of the game. This affects the length of time the
          text comments are displayed at the BOTTOM of the screen. Setting
          the speed to "1" plays the game at the same speed the Amiga uses
          if in it's "Fast Amiga vs Amiga" mode. For players already familiar
          with the game, this helps out when playing on slower systems(One of
          my users requested this one!). Values between 1 and 200 may be used,
          or a "0" may be specified to use the built-in percentage wait limits,
          where shorter lines display for shorter periods of time, etc.

        - If you SAVE the game at the end of a hand, when you RESUME the game,
          I've made the score screen display the "Previous Total" as it would
          have appeared if you had REALLY just completed the hand. (Remember
          that the Grand Total already HAD the Hand Total added to it when
          the game was SAVED, so the user would see the Previous Total as
          being equal to the Grand Total, which made it look like the game
          wasn't adding the points it should have).

        - The "turns" counter is now immediately set to "0" at the end of
          each turn. This was done to correct the Score Bug in SAVED games,
          however it has a little quirk, as when the game is RESUMED, the
          player will see the game screen with a setting of "0 turns" right
          before the screen is cleared to display the Scores Screen. (I know
          it's only on the screen for a second, but want you to be aware of
          it in the event either YOU or one of your USERS spots it and tells
          you it's a BUG or something! hehe)

        - I noted that if a user has the same name for their HANDLE as they
          do for their FIRST NAME, it gets a bit confusing to see who is who,
          so it now appends a " 1" and " 2" to the names in these instances.

        - The game now checks for waiting OLM messages, and a "OLM Waiting"
          message will appear in the lower right corner if one should ever
          be waiting for you. In addition, when this message IS displayed,
          a new OLM command will be available(via the "O" or "8" keys), which
          will clear the screen, allow you to READ and/or RESPOND to the OLM,
          then press ENTER to redisplay the game screen and continue your game
          play. (Thanks to Capt Irgaac for helping me test this feature, even
          though he probably did not know he was! hehe)

        - If you complete a game which you RESUMED, the game now asks you if
          you'd like to DELETE the saved game on file. (This allows you to
          maintain housecleaning chores, as well as allowing you NOT to have
          it automatically delete the game, in the event you wish to finish
          the game again, but possibly try for a different outcome).

>>==--> v1.10 <--==<<   /* To those few who obtained advance copies, please
                           reread this section, as a few new additions were
                           made to this document & the readme file!         */

    Released: December 15, 1996

    Bug Fixes:

        - When a hazard was played on an Amiga Opponent by a HUMAN player,
          they'd Coup Fourre it if possible, but even though they had the
          correct Safety, the text at the bottom would say it had used the
          Extra Tank safety card to block the Safety. (Mike, aka Star Gazer)

        - If you SAVED a game AFTER all cards had been dealt out and AFTER
          at least one hand contained NO MORE cards, an error could occur if
          an Amiga attempted to make a random discard. (Randomly discard
          what?) (Jon & I)

        - If you SAVED a game AFTER obtaining 700 miles, even though it was
          resetting the hand total, it was readding the newly calculated
          hand total to the grand total(which meant you were getting twice
          the points for the hand in your grand total). (Star Gazer)

        - If you(or an Amiga player) were under a Speed Limit, and someone
          tried to STOP you, but you had the Right of Way card, the Speed
          Limit wasn't always "really" removed from the board. Sometimes it
          was even still in effect, however you couldn't End Of Limit either!
          (Jon & I)

        - If you opted to play the EXTENSION, then completed the hand and
          chose to SAVE the game, when you resumed the game, the point total
          screen was not showing the Extension Bonus(because the extension
          wasn't being saved to the SAVED GAME file).

        - During the Point Talley at the end of a HAND, the extension bonus
          was not correctly being added to each players hand total. I had
          thought I fixed this earlier, but noted I had only fixed the DISPLAY
          portion of the routine, but not the actual TALLEY portion.

        - I added a + (plus) sign to the {JL+1} command used in the welcome
          file that loads the SHOW file, as there were some SysOps who also
          had other MCI jump commands in their welcome file that used {1}
          as well(like for a `Quick Logon' type prompt). This OTHER {1} would
          be branched to FIRST, thus forming a loop of redispalying the SHOW
          file OVER and OVER.

        - The Error Check routine received the latest updates.

    Additions/Changes, etc:

        - We now have a Title Screen! Thanks to Jon for creating the screen
          I finally decided to use. Also thanks to the numerous others who
          sent screens for me to consider(some who even sent more than one)!

        - The "Break-In" options for HUMAN player games is now in place! Once
          the game has started, you can press "B" while an AMIGA player is
          taking their turn and be able to do the following:

            - Swap Seats : Able to change seats with any other player at the
                table. This lets you take over their hand and piles, while
                the player you swap with gets YOUR piles and hand. You could
                even swap seats with your partner if you want!

            - Kick Partner : If you opted to play your partners hand when the
                game first started, this lets you drop the reins on your
                partner and the appropriate Amiga player will take over that
                hand for you!

            - Play Partner : If you initially opted to NOT play your partners
                hand, or you kicked your partner out earlier, this command
                lets you once again control the partner's holdings.

            - Take Partner : If, while playing the game, you wish you had
                one of the other Amiga players as your partner, instead of
                the one the "draw" gave you, this command lets you swap your
                current partner with one of the other players. (This only
                works if your original partner was also an Amiga player)

        - If the player opts to use one of the new Break-In options, they
          will NOT be eligable to obtain a High Score!

        - I added a show of the Previous Total right below the Hand Total.
          This "Previous Total" was your grand total BEFORE the points earned
          for the last hand are added to become your NEW Grand Total.

        - If the HAND is won by an Amiga Player, the Amiga will decide if it
          wants to play the Extension or not(when playing a 2, 3, or 6-Player
          Game), as it really should NOT be up to the HUMAN to decide. This
          is especially true where the High Scores are concerned.

        - Note the new default value of the "amigascores" variable is "0",
          instead of "1", so change it back to "1" before using this version
          if you wanted the Amiga eligable for High Scores. The game is
          fairly popular over here, so I wanted the scores files to show the
          names of users, versus amalagams.
          
>>==--> v1.07 <--==<<

    Released: December 9, 1996

    Bug Fixes:

        - Missed a "carrier check" in one location that really needed it. Also
          included checks to various locations, just to speed the exiting of
          the file a bit IF a carrier is lost.(Jon and I both found this one)

        - During the end of deck play, when the Draw Pile was empty, it was
          possible for you to either PLAY or DISCARD from an empty card slot.
          This could give you a slight playing advantage, and should NOT have
          been possible anyways. (Michael, aka Star Gazer found this one)

        - For Numeric Keypad players, it's always been possible to play the
          game using the keypad alone, however Jon noted that the "0" key,
          which was supposed to be an alternate way to DISCARD, did not work
          as advertised. (Jon again)

        - When a HAND is SAVED at the 700/1000 Mile Mark for completion later,
          the next time you PLAYED the game, it would give you TWICE the
          amount of points you were entitled too. Your HandTotal variable is
          now reset to "0" when you continue a game, as it will be recreated
          shortly after restarting anyways. (the "lingering" v1.06 bug!)

        - If a user initially had the MORE prompt enabled, then entered the
          Mille Docs file and dropped carrier, the more prompt was disabled
          the next time they logged on. It will now be reenabled if needed.

        - If at one time you SAVED a game, then used the DELETE command to
          delete the game, when you started a NEW game upon entering the file
          new, if you opted to SAVE the new game, the list of saved entries
          appeared using GAME.1, GAME.2, thru GAME.5, instead of the text
          stating the slots were "Empty". This one was a pup to find! hehe

    Changes/Additions:

        - To complete the FULL availability of using the Numeric Keypad to
          play this game, the following ADDITIONAL keystrokes are now possible
          using either the main keyboard, or the keypad:

                "*" will also activate ReDraw(as well as "R")
                    (This works in ALL play modes.)

                "." (period) will also activate BreakIn (as well as "B")
                    (Remember, though, that BreakIn still only functions
                     inside an Amiga vs Amiga game).

                "8" (or "9") will also ask for QUIT (along with "Q").
                    (This works in ALL play modes.)

        - The request to view the High Scores files was moved BACK in the
          game selection process, so that the only prompt presented on the
          "Hidden Command Mode" screen is the confirmation that you DO want
          to play that game.

        - When SAVING a game, if you select a slot that presently contains a
          SAVED game, you'll be asked if it's alright to overwrite that game
          with the stats of the current game.

        - The SAVE GAME screen now offers a default option of NOT saving the
          game. (In case you went there by mistake)

        - The correct NAME of this game is "Mille Bornes", however I had been
          using "Milles Bornes" (two "s"'s) in numerous places throughout the
          file. "Mille Bornes" stands for "Mile Stones", however my usage of
          "CNetMilles" (WITH the "s") IS correct, as that meaning converts to
          "CNet Miles", which IS what I wanted it to say. So, this means that
          within the GAME and the DOCS, references to the game should NOT have
          the "s" after "Mille", but when dealing with the directory and file
          names used IN the game, the "s" SHOULD come after "Mille". :-) I
          know, it's my "perfectionist" nature showing through again! hehe

        - The High Scores still weren't functioning the way I initially had
          envisioned them. They now work in this manner: The player must get
          a Score HIGHER than one of the ones on the list ALREADY in order to
          get added TO the list. In past versions, if your score MATCHED one
          of the scores on the list, and there were available spots IN the
          list, then you'd get added. This resulted in having many entries
          with the SAME Mileage value on the list, which I didn't think was
          appropriate. One interesting side effect of the way I'm doing it
          now is that you have to BEAT a score(even if it's the SAME score as
          yours, so if User 1 is on the list with 1500 miles, and he's number
          2 on the list under another user with, say 2200 miles, even if YOU
          score 1500 miles, you WILL NOT be added to the list, even though the
          list only contained those 2 users, because you FAILED to BEAT the
          score of User 2). I hope that's understandable to you all. :-)

        - The "Ron Amiga" player was renamed to "Ray Amiga" in rememberence
          of a close family friend who passed away Friday, December 6, 1996
          of a Brain Aneurysm. He was 46 years of age and has left a wife and
          three children(the youngest of which is in 7th grade). Please hold
          a place in your hearts or prayers for this family during the holiday
          season. 

>>==--> v1.06 <--==<<

    Released: December 7, 1996 (Moving right along! hehe)

    Bug Fixes: (Thanks to Jon Reddick for finding & reporting many of these*)

       *- At the completion of a hand, the "# of Safeties" talley was being
          calculated wrong for player 2 and 3. (Actually, instead of adding
          their safeties, I was adding player 1's safeties to all three!)

       *- My "random player seating" routine was being called at the end of
          every hand. This was extremely bad, as you'd lose yourself or the
          players you were just getting used to playing against. Ooops! hehe

       *- If you completed a game by scoring EXACTLY 5,000 miles, then it
          didn't accept that as a win and would, in fact, ask if you'd like
          to play the next hand! This was fixed!

        - There were some problems in the way the game SAVED high scores,
          where it would sometimes show incorrect variable data.

        - Because I wasn't SAVING the "old" values of the Battle and Speed
          piles, and because I was not using separate "old" piles for ALL
          players(but in fact was only using ONE set of "old" piles), there
          were times when you'd play a "Coup Fourre" or a "Prevention", and
          would notice that what you USED to have underneath the Hazard is
          NOT what was shown when the Hazard WAS removed. In order to fix
          this problem, all SAVED GAME files need to be DELETED again!

        - Numerous additional color mistakes and typos were found & fixed!

    Changes/Additions:

        - Colorization of the Docs file is now complete! Check out the "New
          & Improved" Rule Book section!

        - Loss of Carrier checks were added to both prompts in the Docs file.

        - The new "CNetMilles.Show" file is complete! Placed in your welcome
          file and a random High Score file will be shown to your users and
          they will be asked if they wish to play the game!

        - Program consolidation is complete! The main file is now 46,315 bytes
          long(from 78,??? bytes previously). The file has yet to be optimized
          but this should make SOME of you happier!

        - The SHOWTXT() routine used to display the lines of text at the
          bottom of the main game screen received a slight change that you'll
          immediately notice, because it gives us a 40% increase in display
          speed of these text strings!

        - To show how fast the computer can REALLY play, if you choose to
          WATCH the Amiga play against itself, you'll additionally be asked
          if you want it to slow the pace down enough for you to read, or if
          it can play out the game at it's own speed. On my 2000/020, it's so
          fast I can just make out what the lines of text are saying, but
          forget comparing that info to what was printed to the game screen
          above. On a 4000/040 or better, you can forget even trying to read
          the text lines I'm willing to bet! hehe

        - The "Joy Amiga" player was renamed "Jen Amiga", as some of you were
          thinking this was "Joe Amiga", but a typo somewhere in the code.

        - The Player NAMES have been added to the "Hand/Game Score Talley"
          screen to make reading the screen a bit easier.

        - Two more information pieces have been added to the main game screen:

            - Turns: Tells you how many "rounds" have been completed in the
                     current hand. If you opt to SAVE the game, this turns
                     variable is also SAVED.

            - Cards: Tells you how many cards are left in the Draw Pile. Even
                     though you wouldn't know EXACTLY how many cards were left
                     in the REAL game, you could get an IDEA as to how many
                     were left simply by noting how thick the stack was!

        - If you reload a game saved previously, then choose to SAVE the game
          again, you'll be told which slot the game was previously saved in.
          This comes in real handy if you have numerous 2-Player games saved,
          and are trying to SAVE a 2-player game, for instance. (This was in
          the last version, but I forgot to mention it! hehe)

    - A Lingering Bug:

        - There's a bug in the SAVED GAMES scoring if you choose to SAVE the
          game at the END of a hand. The next time you reload the game, you'll
          be told you've WON the hand(which is normal), however if the SCORE
          you obtained in the hand is 2,500 points or higher, you'll ALSO be
          told that you've won the GAME as well, and will have your name(s)
          added to the High Score files for the GAME, too. This, with the
          additional fact that your name is added to the High Hand file again
          as well still exist in the coding, but I wanted(needed) to get the
          current version out to you as promised! I'll catch this bug in the
          next release. :-)

>>==--> v1.05 <--==<<

    Released: December 4, 1996 (only 2 people downloaded 1.04)

    Bug Fixes:

        - Unless you were the HIGHEST numbered player in the game, when you
          SAVED a game, all the data wasn't being saved. ie: if you were the
          third player in a 6 player game, players 4, 5, and 6 would not get
          SAVED. Due to this bug, all saved games will once again need to be
          deleted and restarted.

        - I slightly <over> corrected when fixing the HUMAN side, when you
          wanted to play a Hazard on a player who was showing a Right of Way
          card. Opponents got a "super" Right of Way card, because it would
          not let you play ANY of the Hazard cards on them! (Thank Star Gazer
          for finding this one.)

>>==--> v1.04 <--==<<

    Released: December 4, 1996

    Bug Fixes: 

        - Put simply, the "Right of Way" card was royally screwed up! hehe
          There were NUMEROUS places where the computer (and two places where
          the human) player was NOT taking this card into consideration, or
          else considering the card incorrectly. Hopefully, these problems
          have all been addressed in this version. (I've been testing this
          version CONSIDERABLY on this end the past few week!)

        - There were some problems in the SAVE GAME coding if you saved the
          game right after reaching the 700 mile(or the 1000 mile) mark. Now
          if you hit 700 and SAVE, when resuming, you'll immediately be asked
          if you'd like to play the extension, while if resuming at 1000, it
          will immediately ask if you'd like to play the next hand.

        - When playing a PARTNER game(either 4 or 6 players), the amiga would
          play HAZARD cards on it's PARTNER! If only that were possible! :-)

        - In some instances, the amiga would play a Speed Limit card on one
          of it's opponents, and either the Speed Limit would NOT show up, or
          was being placed on the WRONG Speed Pile(related to the bug above)!

    Changes/Additions:

        - High Scores files are now created for each TYPE of game(2, 3, 4, or
          6-Players) and for both the High HAND as well as the High GAME. I
          will also be including a small ARexx file for use in your welcome
          file that will display these files to each user while logging on,
          and will also have the ability of launching the game then and there!
          (Will include in the next release version)

        - I've begun the process of CONSOLIDATING the coding. This means going
          through and recoding sections to remove redundant code wherever
          possible. The file is still blitzed with /* Commenting */, however
          is beginning to shrink considerably. Where the original v1.00 was
          78K, this version is 66K in length(and that INCLUDES the above NEW
          coding for the High Scores routines). Eventually, once the bugs are
          all taken care of, I'll go in and begin OPTIMIZING the code, however
          I'll also include a "source" listing in CONSOLIDATED format for any
          SysOp interested in taking a gander at the READABLE form.

        - The lines of text printed to the bottom of the screen telling you
          what player did what were changed slightly from the HUMAN side, as
          when playing a PARTNER game and you opt to also play the PARTNER,
          it was hard to tell WHICH partner did what. The HUMAN text lines
          now also include the NAME of the player who does something, just
          like the AMIGA players use.

        - The AMOUNT OF TIME the above mentioned text lines appear on the
          screen was not enough to be readable I was told. After initially
          doubling this value, it occurred to me that the LENGTH of the text
          line should ALSO play a role in how long it was displayed, so this
          routine has been modified to present the info for a set period of
          time based on the LENGTH of the line. For the longer lines, this 
          period is roughly 1.8 to 2 times longer than the previous version,
          while the shorter lines may be .8 to the original length wait. I'm
          still playing with this some, so if you feel it's STILL displaying
          too fast, please get back to me.

        - Since the release of the initial BETA version, there have been SIX,
          unique AMIGA opponents in the game, EACH with their own playing
          style, however these opponents were "locked" into finite positions
          within the game. By this, I mean that if playing a 2-Player game,
          you'd ALWAYS be playing against the SECOND Amiga Opponent. Through
          a bit more coding, I'm now "shuffling" the Amiga Opponents BEFORE
          assigning them to their seats. This will allow you the possibility
          of going against any one of the six Amiga opponents in ANY game,
          and will allow you to PARTNER yourself with an Amiga Player with
          different styles of play. One of you(Mike), has asked if it could
          be possible to SPECIFY which player you went up against, so that,
          if you were a seasoned expert at the game, you could go against a
          cutthroat version player. Upon thinking about this, I believe that,
          in real life, this would NOT happen. There would be times you'd be
          playing against a player NOT as good as you, and othertimes against
          a player that could play your socks off. IF, however, you feel that
          a feature like this SHOULD be added to the game, let me know. If a
          feature of this type WERE to be included, I'm make it an option for
          the USER, so THEY could specify which player they played against.

        - The GROWCARD() routine was modified to create the proper preceeding
          word in front of the card NAME. Either "a" or "an", depending on if
          the card started with a vowel, or the word "the" if one of the four
          Safety Cards is used(as there is only ONE of EACH in the deck). MCI
          coding was also removed to slightly speed up the display of these
          text lines as well.

        - Numerous internal color changes were made.

>>==--> v1.00 <--==<<

    Released: November 28, 1996 (w/ BETA versions starting 11/5/95)

    After numerous BETA releases, this first version of the game was released
to the general masses.

    Thanks to Paloma, Galt, and Star Gazer for their BETA TESTING efforts and
useful bug reports.

    Things lacking in the initial release included the high scores files,
the ability to break into the game if you were already playing(meaning it was
only possible in a computer controlled game), and a few other internal coding
considerations, like consolidated code and optimized code.

